1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
I'm James Longo and I am a troop committee chairman with Troop 228 in Rockford, Michigan.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast is sponsored by backers like me.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And now the old Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Hey, I have a theory I want to share with you. I think Santa is a scouter.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Yeah, because he has a beard, wears a uniform, drives an ancient vehicle and his clothes are all tarnished with ashes and soot.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
What do you think?

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Hey, this is podcast number 297.. Hey,

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast. This is Clark Green. Let's take a look in the mailbag

9
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
We heard from Scouter James, who's with the 4th Chatham Scout Troop in Chatham, Ontario, Canada.

10
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
He said lots of good stuff in the PDF package I just got.

11
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
I've posted a few around the Scout Hall for our parents' visit.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
My general theme is: ask not what you can do for your Scouts, Ask what your Scouts can do for themselves.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Things like: it's not your backpack, it's theirs, Let them pack it.

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
It's good to know, James, that our brothers to the North encounter the same difficulties that we do.

15
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And James goes on to say: also, the parents have finally stopped asking me what the Scouts are doing, because my response has been:

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
I don't know. have you asked your Scout? And they say: but he doesn't know.

17
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
And I say, well, has he asked his patrol leader?

18
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Ah yes, Simple yet effective answers. Thanks for getting in touch, James.

19
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:05,000
John Ruth is wearing several hats. He's volunteering for the Seneca District of the Great Trail Council in Akron, Ohio.

20
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
And John wrote in to say I returned to Scouting this year after 40 years. in the quote wilderness unquote.

21
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
I earned my Eagle and aged out in 1975..

22
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
I found your website a month or so ago and I finished my first run through your podcast archive in 19 days.

23
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Wow, John, that's got to be a record.

24
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Anybody else? Have you gotten through the back log of podcasts in anything less than 19 days? I doubt it.

25
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,000
I found this auditory track, or death march perhaps, was very rewarding.

26
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,000
I agree with your emphasis on asking questions on the patrol method and the Scout-led troop,

27
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
which is really Baden Powell's original vision for Scouting.

28
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
I also appreciate your emphasis on advancement by doing what Scouts do

29
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:56,000
and on judging a Scout's progress by asking how far he's come, not how far he has left to go.

30
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
As I listened to each podcast I thought back over my time in Scouting.

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
I'd have to say that my Scout troop, troop 170 in Fremont, California,

32
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,000
probably was more adult than youth-led, but with enough left to the patrols that a good amount of creative chaos remained.

33
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:17,000
It is clear that Scout-led Scouting is a valuable antidote to many of today's schools,

34
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
and directing Scouts energy and natural curiosity so they learn for themselves

35
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
is superior to imposing artificial constraints and requiring only the repetition of assigned course material.

36
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Put another way, Scout-led Discovery beats adult-led efficiency.

37
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
That's a great thought, John. I really like that.

38
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Scout-led Discovery beats adult-led efficiency.

39
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
John went on to say thanks for all you do.

40
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:49,000
The only thing more remarkable than your dedication to Scouting is the stantyma you display in keeping ScoutmasterCGcom humming.

41
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
May it continue to be blessed in both regards.

42
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Well, thank you so much.

43
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:01,000
And John also sent me a number of reflections on the technical end of the podcast and I really do appreciate that.

44
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Our old pal, Frank Maynard, is a unit commissioner in the mighty Ottawa district in Novi Michigan

45
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:13,000
and he wrote to say I like the way you talked about cooperative volunteering in podcast 294

46
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
and how lots of adults in troops aren't really that good of a thing.

47
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:24,000
I was looking back through some of our troops, old rosters, and drew a casual correlation between the quality of the program,

48
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:31,000
whether it was youth run and Scouts actually responsible for things and the ratio of adults to Scouts.

49
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
I mean it makes sense, doesn't it, Frank?

50
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
If there's a lot of adults involved, Scouts don't really have that much of a fighting chance,

51
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
because adults, you know they want to do something.

52
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Frank went on to say: isn't it curious that we usually have a shortage of adult help at the Cub Scout level

53
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,000
but then suddenly volunteers come out of the woodwork when their son crosses into the troop.

54
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Yeah, it is kind of curious

55
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:58,000
And I think it's a really good idea for experienced Scoutsers to go offer their help to the Cub Scout pack,

56
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
because you'll form a bridge right between the Cub Scout pack and the Scout troop

57
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
and the Venture crew and everything else.

58
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
I think it's a great idea for you to do.

59
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Thanks for being in touch, Frank.

60
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Most weeks we try and have a couple of live chat sessions over at ScoutMasterCGcom.

61
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Watch the Facebook feed and the Twitter feed for announcements when those happen.

62
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
The standard times are Tuesday and Wednesday mornings.

63
