You'll find a number of options on making a voluntary one-time subscription payment to keep things up and running.

87
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:08,000
And, as promised, I'd like to take a moment this week to personally thank Gary Bezal and Aaron Kohler,

88
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,000
who've become backers since our last podcast.

89
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Go to ScoutMasterCGcom, click the support link at the top of the page,

90
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Become a backer and I will thank you during our next podcast.

91
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Well, in this week's podcast, I got one simple thing for you.

92
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
It's in ScoutMasterCG in seven minutes or less.

93
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:27,000
I'm kind of excited about it

94
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,000
And that's going to take up the remainder of this brief podcast.

95
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
So let's get started. shall we

96
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Scout MasterCG in seven minutes or less.

97
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:36,000
For nearly six years, as I've recorded the podcast- and it's more like 10 or 11 years that the blog has been going-

98
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
I've been writing and thinking about scouting

99
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:45,000
And one of my little pet projects has been trying to reduce everything that we do down to a couple of sentences.

100
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
But I've got news for you.

101
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:51,000
I think I came up with one sentence that works,

102
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
And I'm going to explain why.

103
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,000
Because it's a lot easier to memorize one sentence than like a whole bunch of gobbledygook,

104
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:04,000
And if I can impress upon you the import of that, then you have something that's useful for you, right?

105
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,000
That's actually has some utility for you as a scouter.

106
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:13,000
So I want to take a little time and explain exactly what I'm talking about.

107
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,000
If you follow my reasoning, scouters have one aim and one method.

108
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
That's a little radical, but bear with me for a moment.

109
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
See if this makes sense.

110
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Now. aim is a particularly well-chosen word to describe our focus as adult volunteers in scouting.

111
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
It's the word that we inherit from Baden Powell: the aim of scouting-

112
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,000
What is scouting supposed to do?

113
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:43,000
And a lot of times we hear that scouting has three specific objectives that are commonly referred to as the aims of scouting,

114
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
And they are character development, citizenship training and personal fitness.

115
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Now I'm going to take a little leap here and reduce that list to one aim,

116
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
And here's why

117
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:01,000
An ideal person of good character is going to be a good citizen and is going to keep themselves fit.

118
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:11,000
I think that we can reasonably say we have one aim, and that is to shape individuals of good character.

119
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:16,000
Now, I'm not saying that citizenship and personal fitness aren't important, but you understand what I'm saying, right.

120
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:20,000
I'm not saying that we erase those or we don't think about them.

121
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:27,000
If we're going to make this easy to remember and we're going to make it something that is a tool that you can use,

122
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:32,000
that you can actually apply, let's get it down to one aim

123
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
And talk about aim for a moment. okay,

124
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Because an aim is a purpose or intention towards which one's efforts are directed.

125
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,000
As a shooting sports director at our camp years ago, I learned a lot about aiming.

126
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:50,000
It's not as simple as leveling a bow or a rifle at a target and hoping for the best.

127
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:55,000
The goal of a good shooter is developing consistency in their aim and the execution of the shot.

128
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:02,000
Now, when an arrow leaves the bow and travel towards a target, it's subject to a number of forces that affect its flight and where it hits the target,

129
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
And it's really kind of interesting to study.

130
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,000
If you come down to the archery range at camp and you're of scout age,

131
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:16,000
you are not at first really interested in finding out like all of these fascinating things about what happens to an arrow after it leaves the bow

132
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
and how what you do affects that.

133
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:23,000
What you're interested in doing is shooting as many arrows towards a target as you possibly can,

134
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
And that's the way we all start right

135
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
When we're volunteering for scouts. we want to go, man,

136
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
We want to do stuff.

137
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Please don't make me sit under the tarp here and listen to you talk about the physics of archery

138
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,000
or how scouting works.

139
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Please, let me get out there and do some stuff.

140
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
Well, and that's fine.

141
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:51,000
You go out and you do some stuff and you gain some experience. and that experience reveals that you need to know more stuff,

142
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:55,000
That you need to spend some time perfecting your aim.

143
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:00,000
When guys came down to the archery range at camp, it was the idea was: okay, I know you want to shoot,

144
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Let's go out and shoot.

145
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
Here's the simple safety rules,

146
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,000
Here's the way to get you started.

147
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Let's get out and start shooting

148
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
And then, once they had some experience with it, then you could actually explain things right

149
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:17,000
And you could actually get somewhere, because you could say, okay, well, here's the different steps in aiming

150
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:21,000
And this is how to work it, and that will get you a better result.

151
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
And I think it's exactly the same way with scouting,

152
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:29,000
You have to go out and you have to try some things out and you get a little bit of experience and you go:

153
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:34,000
wow, that worked really well. but the other thing that I did didn't work all that well.

154
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:40,000
I better study up a little bit and see if there's anything that I'm doing wrong or things that I can do better.

155
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
So back to my metaphor:

156
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:46,000
An arrow's flight depends wholly on how it's aimed and released,

157
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:54,000
And a scout's progress and our effectiveness is largely dependent on how well we know and focus on the aim

158
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
And then we apply the methods.

159
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,000
We know what the aim is.

160
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:58,000
It's the center of the target.

161
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:02,000
We got that, but now we need some methods to get the arrow into that target.

162
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:07,000
If, for some reason, you had never seen a bow and an arrow before, you've never seen anybody shoot it,

163
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:11,000
and I handed you a bow and an arrow and I said, go ahead, put that in a target,

164
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:18,000
Well, you would have to do an awful lot of experimenting, probably before you got even close to the target

165
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:25,000
And in the end, given the time, you'd find out the method of making that happen.

166
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
So what are the methods of scouting?

167
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:31,000
If you do a quick Google search, you'll find lots of lists of them

168
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
And most of them are in common.

169
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Most of them are like standard BSA methods and you can find them repeated in other scouting organizations and things like that

170
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:48,000
Slightly different wording. but I think we can reduce the whole list down to one method

171
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:52,000
And if this isn't the first podcast you've listened to, you know what that method is. right,

172
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:53,000
It's the patrol method.

173
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
Well, what about all the other method?

174
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
I thought we were supposed to balance everything, right.

175
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:01,000
I thought we had like eight methods that were supposed to balance all equally.

176
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:02,000
Well, no,

177
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:07,000
No, there's one method out of which all the other methods flow.

178
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:12,000
That is the way that we gain the other results, and that's the patrol method.

179
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:18,000
It's kind of like I was saying at the beginning: we can take the three aims of scouting and reduce them down to one.

180
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:31,000
We can take all the methods of scouting which, if you want the list, okay, it's: ideals, patrols, outdoor programs, advancement, association with adults, personal growth, leadership development and the uniform.

181
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:39,000
If we take all eight of those, the one method that assures we're going to get all eight of those things is the patrol method,

182
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:46,000
And I guess I could spend a lot of time going over each one of them and explaining exactly what I mean by that. but I think you get it right.

183
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:56,000
So now I found my one-sentence job description, you know one-sentence mission statement for every scouter everywhere.

184
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:57,000
Here it is:

185
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:01,000
Are you ready to shape character by applying the patrol method?

186
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:02,000
Ta-da,

187
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:06,000
So you can memorize that

188
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:11,000
My job as a scouter is to shape character by applying the patrol method.

189
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
It's pretty easy to understand.

190
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:20,000
That's really the starting line and the finish line. the whole McGillow, the whole Bollowax, the one single test of what we are doing.

191
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:24,000
Can we draw a line back to shaping character using the patrol method?

192
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:25,000
Then that's scouting.

193
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
If we can't, it isn't.

194
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:30,000
I know that's a bold statement, isn't it?

195
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,000
But I like bold statements, don't you?

196
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:38,000
No, but I really be curious to hear your reflections on that.

197
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:49,000
And if you are not working with a scout troop, if you're working with a cub pack or a venturing crew, you're working in rovers or weeblows or beavers or any of those types of groups.

198
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:55,000
all those groups work by an age-appropriate application of the patrol method.

199
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
Don't let the word patrol throw you.

200
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:02,000
A beaver colony is going to use the patrol method at an age-appropriate level, right?

201
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:07,000
A cub scout den is going to use the patrol method at an age-appropriate level.

202
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:13,000
Rovers are going to use the patrol method at an age-appropriate level as our venturers, as our scouts.

203
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:19,000
So our job as scouters is to shape character by applying the patrol method.

204
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:20,000
It's that simple,

205
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
That's the key,

206
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:32,000
And every key that unlocks a door and every simple statement of course, opens into a whole world of other things to study and to understand.

207
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:36,000
So you know, don't think that we're done once we have that down,

208
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
But that's the starting line.

209
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:43,000
That's where we begin to open the understanding of this wonderful game called scouting.

210
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:48,000
Your job as a scouter is to develop character by applying the patrol method.

211
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:49,000
Pretty cool, huh?

212
00:16:49,000 --> 00:17:11,000
Well, thanks so much for listening to this 297th edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast.

